# The Founderizer release public keys (PRODUCT.md 4.2 and 4.3).
#
# One base64 line for each 32-byte Ed25519 public key, and lines that start with # are for the reader. The private
# halves live only on the website host that issues releases, and never on a hub: a fleet can check a release and
# can never make one.
#
# **This file holds a set, and every key in it is trusted.** That is what makes a key replaceable: the release
# that brings the next key is signed with the key it replaces, every release after it is signed with the new one,
# and a fleet that skipped the release in between still carries a key that checks both. PRODUCT.md 4.3, "How a
# signing key is replaced", says how a key is brought in, how long both stay here and when the old line goes.
# At most four lines; a line that is not a 32-byte key makes the whole file carry nothing, because a key file that
# is half read is a mistake and a fleet that cannot say which keys it trusts takes no release.
#
# The first release must publish **two** keys: the one that signs it and a spare whose private half is kept off
# the website host. Without that spare, a lost private key ends the update path of every fleet in the field, and
# the only way out is a console on each customer's machine.
#
# This file carries no key yet, because P7 (the shop and the channel) makes the key pairs on that host and commits
# the public halves here. Until then `releases.read_keys` answers an empty set, every manifest is refused with
# "this checkout carries no release key", and no fleet can be updated from the channel. That is the safe state,
# not a gap: a fleet that cannot check a release takes none.
